  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda street child reintegration, foster care and family support</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Large cities throughout Africa act as a magnet for children whose parents can no longer provide the care and love they need. Uganda is no exception, with thousands of children surviving on the streets, many with no home to return to at night. There are many girls and very young children who are particularly vulnerable to abuse. Small numbers of children are taken into care, but the scale of the problem requires new thinking and direction. This project will build on a successful fostering initiative, to increase the ability of communities to take responsibility for these children. Organisations with previous experience of fostering, with work with girls, and with supporting families to build small businesses, will come together to share skills and develop effective ways to support more street children. In this way, the most vulnerable street children will be given a stable and caring environment alongside long term support into education and training for work. The project will focus on children under the age of 17, living full time on the street &#x2013; taking them right through to living back in their community. Importantly, all three organisations that are part of this project will share learning and develop ways to work with significantly more street children than is currently the case. Retrak has designed an innovative programme of collaboration with two other Ugandan agencies to develop work on fostering, reaching more street girls, and addressing the needs of street children in a more holistic way. This will involve supporting families and carers as well as the children, and expanding contacts with other service providers.</narrative>
  </description>
